# tsk git-backend: notes from a read-through

Took a pass through `noah/git-backend-2` — really nice rewrite,
the content-addressed model is the right call and the module
boundaries are clean. A few things stood out that seem worth
thinking about while the design is still settling. Not a security
audit so much as design feedback; some of these are probably
already on your list.

I'm assuming you want the integrity story to actually hold up
against an untrusted remote — i.e., a teammate with push access
to the shared origin shouldn't be able to silently rewrite tasks
on my clone. If that's not the threat model you're targeting yet,
some of these become non-issues.

## The big one: what does "stable id" actually promise?

The architecture doc says stable id = SHA of the root content
blob, and `patch.rs` says "tampered patches are rejected." Both
imply content immutability, but the implementation lands somewhere
in between immutable and mutable in a way that I don't think is
intentional.

In `patch::import_one_chain`:

```rust
if idx == 0 {
    if content_oid.to_string() != stable_hex {
        return Err(...);
    }
}
```

The check only fires on the first commit. Every later commit's
`content` blob is taken on faith. And `object::read` reads the
*tip* commit's tree, not the root's. So an mbox with one valid
root entry and one tampered follow-up entry imports cleanly and
`tsk show` returns the attacker's bytes.

The `tamper_detected_via_stable_id_check` test passes because it
only exercises a single-commit chain — the multi-commit case
isn't covered.

`git-pull` is a stronger version of the same thing: there's no
content check at all. `merge_strategy` runs `merge_trees` and
writes whatever comes out; `rebase_strategy` replays onto the
remote tip; the `(None, Some(r))` arm just copies the remote ref
verbatim. So anyone who can push to `refs/tsk/*` on the shared
origin can rewrite any task on every other clone.

I think you have two coherent options here, and the current code
accidentally implements neither cleanly:

**Option A: content is immutable, properties carry mutation.**
Stable id stays SHA-of-root. Every commit on the chain has the
same `content` blob (verified). Edits happen by adding/changing
properties — which is already how `status` works. This matches
what the docs say and what the `new_task` reopen-on-duplicate
logic suggests.

**Option B: content is mutable, stable id is just a birth-name.**
Then drop the integrity language from the docs. Anyone who pushes
to a task ref can change its body, intentionally. You'd want some
other mechanism — signed commits, a separate audit ref, *something* —
if you still want the multi-user story to be safe.

A is much closer to where you are. The fix is small: hoist that
check out of the `if idx == 0` guard so it runs on every commit
in the chain, and add the same check at the boundaries in
`merge.rs` (after each reconciliation arm produces a candidate
tip, verify the tip's content blob hashes to the stable id; if
not, leave the local ref alone and surface a `Rejected` outcome).

A `tsk verify` subcommand that walks every task ref's chain and
reports mismatches would basically fall out of the same code, and
you could fold it into `tsk fix-up`. Worth it as a regression
guard for whichever option you pick.

## Why the host doesn't help you

I went to check whether Tangled's permission model could backstop
this — surely `refs/tsk/*` could be protected somehow — and the
answer is no, and won't be unless they add per-ref permissions to
the RBAC layer.

Reading `core/rbac/rbac.go`, the policy grain is
`(subject, domain, repo, action)` with actions like `repo:push`,
`repo:settings`, `repo:owner`. There's no per-ref or per-refspace
discrimination — once a user has `repo:push`, they can push any
ref in the repo. The SSH guard (`core/guard/guard.go`) authorizes
on `(user, repo, gitCommand)` and then invokes `git-receive-pack`
directly; there's no `pre-receive` hook layer exposed.

This is the same story on every other host you might mirror to
(GitHub, Forgejo, GitLab) — branch protection only covers
`refs/heads/*` and tag protection only covers `refs/tags/*`. So
the integrity story really does have to live in the tsk client.

Worth a one-paragraph "trust model" section in
`ARCHITECTURE.md` saying something like "tsk treats every fetched
ref as untrusted until verified locally; the server is not a
trust boundary." It changes how someone reads the rest of the
architecture.

(Also worth filing an upstream issue on tangled.org/core
requesting per-refspace permissions in the RBAC layer. Probably
not a tsk-blocker, but defense-in-depth would help any project
using custom refspaces, not just yours. Small upstream, your
issue probably gets read.)

## Terminal escape passthrough in `task::parse`

`task::parse` walks content character by character into the
output buffer, decorates with `colored`, and the various
print sites send the result straight to stdout — no filtering of
C0 / ESC bytes. So a task body containing
`\x1b]0;...\x07` retitles the terminal, OSC 8 produces hyperlinks
where displayed text disagrees with the URL, OSC 52 writes to the
clipboard on permissive terminals, and so on.

`tsk list` is the worst offender because it prints titles for the
whole queue on a routine command and bypasses `task::parse`
entirely (it formats `entry.title` directly). You don't even need
to navigate to the malicious task for the payload to fire.

This is the kind of thing that's only exploitable to the extent
that *something else* (the integrity stuff above) lets attacker
bytes into a task body. So if you fix that, this becomes
self-poisoning only — much less interesting. Still worth doing
though, because:

- `tsk import` of an mbox you got from someone is its own
  attacker-controlled channel even after the integrity story is
  fixed (a maliciously-authored mbox can have any content under a
  legitimate stable id; the stable id verifies that the *content*
  matches the *id*, not that either is benign).
- A small `sanitize_for_display` helper that maps C0 / DEL /
  unpaired ESC to `U+FFFD` and gets called on every span sourced
  from task content is maybe ten lines and avoids needing to
  think about it again.

The `-R` raw flag on `tsk show` is fine to leave unsanitized as
an opt-in.

## Smaller things

A few other things I noticed; flagging in case any of them are
useful but none of them are critical:

**StableId is a newtype around an arbitrary string.** Every
construction site outside `object::create` takes external data
verbatim — `merge.rs`, `queue.rs`, `namespace.rs`, `patch.rs`,
`properties.rs`. None check that it's 40 chars of hex. Probably
fine in practice (git's ref-format rules block the worst
characters, and human ids resolve through namespace bindings
anyway), but a fallible `StableId::parse(s)` constructor would
make the integrity invariant locally checkable wherever stable
ids cross a trust boundary. Folds naturally into the same change
as the integrity fix.

**`tsk -q <name>` and `read_selector` skip name validation.**
The write paths all revalidate via `queue::validate_name` /
`namespace::validate_name`, and the read paths fail safely
(git2 rejects malformed refnames), so I don't think this is
exploitable today. But it's a "next person to add a code path
forgets to revalidate and the gap punches through" situation.
Validating in `Workspace::queue()` / `Workspace::namespace()`
before returning would make it not a thing.

**Argument injection on remote names.** `git push <remote>` and
`git fetch <remote>` are invoked without `--end-of-options`. A
remote name beginning with `-` would be treated as a flag.
`set_default_remote` already validates against `git remote`
output for the persisted path, so this only bites the explicit
CLI argument paths. Two-line fix per call site:
`cmd.args(["push", "--end-of-options", remote, ...])`. Not a
local RCE primitive — `--upload-pack=` and `--receive-pack=` run
on the remote side — but it does enable surprising flag
interpretation. Pure hardening.

**`namespace::next` parser falls back to 1 on parse failure.**
If a remote plants an unparseable `next` blob, the local clone
would treat it as 1 and start re-allocating ids. The merge
driver in `merge.rs:319-321` already re-derives `next` from
`max(mapping.keys()) + 1`, so the merge path is self-healing —
but the `(None, Some(r))` arm at `merge.rs:280-283` copies the
remote ref verbatim without the merge driver. One-line fix to
apply the same re-derivation there.

**`from_utf8_lossy` on remote-supplied blobs.** `object::read`,
`namespace::read_at_commit`, `queue::read_at_commit` all use the
lossy form. Display paths can keep it; the parsing-from-remote
paths probably want strict `from_utf8` so a malicious remote
can't silently mutate a byte sequence into `U+FFFD` on round
trip. `patch::parse_entry` is already inconsistent — strict for
file bodies, lossy for size headers — worth picking one.

**`open` crate is in `Cargo.toml` but not used yet.** When you
wire it up for `ParsedLink::External`, allowlist `https`/`http`
only and confirm before spawning. `url::Url::parse` accepts
`javascript:`, `file:`, `gopher:`, etc., none of which you want
to hand to `xdg-open`.

**`Cargo.toml` repository URL is stale** — points at codeberg,
actual repo is on tangled. (Also: README says "Task-level
metadata requires Linux's xattr(7) API" but I don't see any
`xattr::` calls in the source. Either it got removed or the
docs are ahead of the code.)

## tl;dr

If I had to rank these in the order I'd actually fix them:

1. Decide what stable id means (option A above is much closer to
   where you are), enforce it in `patch.rs` per-commit and at
   every reconciliation arm in `merge.rs`. This is the one that
   actually matters for the multi-user story.
2. Add a `sanitize_for_display` helper for `task::parse` and
   `command_list`. Small, and mbox import remains an exposure
   even after #1.
3. The smaller items are mostly polish — `--end-of-options`,
   `StableId::parse`, the `next` re-derivation in the empty-local
   arm. Probably batch into one defense-in-depth pass.

The architecture is solid. None of this is a "stop everything"
finding — it's the kind of thing that's much easier to get right
during the rewrite than after, which is why I figured I'd flag
it now while you're still in the design phase.

Happy to dig into any of these further, or look at the patches
once you've taken a swing at the integrity stuff if that's
useful.

